label("$X$",(0,0),NW);
[/asy] Since $\angle BXC=70^\circ,$ then $\angle AXC = 180^\circ - \angle BXC = 110^\circ.$

Since $\angle AXC = 110^\circ,$ then $\angle DXA = 180^\circ - \angle AXC = 70^\circ.$

Therefore, $x=\boxed{70}.$

(Alternatively, we could note that when two lines intersect, the vertically opposite angles are equal so $\angle DXA=\angle BXC =70^\circ.$)

question:
There are nine unit triangles in the three rows of this triangle. If you add three more rows to the bottom of this triangle so that each row has 2 more unit triangles than the previous row, how many unit triangles will there be?

[asy]
draw((0,0)--(15,26)--(30,0)--cycle);
draw((5,8.7)--(10,0)--(20,17.3));
draw((20,0)--(25,8.7)--(5,8.7));
draw((20,0)--(10,17.3)--(20,17.3));
[/asy]

ref_solution:
The common ratio between consecutive terms is $\frac{3}{5}$ (you can choose any two consecutive terms and divide the second one by the first to find the common ratio). So the $n^\text{th}$ term of the sequence is $\frac{125}{9} \cdot \left( \frac{3}{5} \right)^{n-1}$. Plugging in $n=8$, we get $$
\frac{125}{9} \cdot \left( \frac{3}{5} \right)^{7} = \frac{5^3}{3^2} \cdot \frac{3^7}{5^7}
= \frac{3^5}{5^4}
= \boxed{\frac{243}{625}}.
$$

