First, I recognize that a rotation centered at the origin can be represented by a rotation matrix. The general form of a rotation matrix is:

\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos\theta & -\sin\theta \\
\sin\theta & \cos\theta
\end{pmatrix}
\]

Given that the vector \(\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}\) is rotated to \(\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -12 \end{pmatrix}\), I can set up the following equations:

\[
5 = 13\cos\theta
\]
\[
-12 = 13\sin\theta
\]

Solving for \(\cos\theta\) and \(\sin\theta\), I find:

\[
\cos\theta = \frac{5}{13}
\]
\[
\sin\theta = -\frac{12}{13}
\]

With these values, the rotation matrix becomes:

\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{5}{13} & \frac{12}{13} \\
-\frac{12}{13} & \frac{5}{13}
\end{pmatrix}
\]

Finally, applying this matrix to the vector \(\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\) gives:

\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{5}{13} & \frac{12}{13} \\
-\frac{12}{13} & \frac{5}{13}
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
